#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" :question/math-academy-id "e-597" :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/assets/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" :question/math-academy-id "e-598" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/assets/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "72076ddb-4a3a-530f-854c-7b1400cd9859" :question/math-academy-id "e-6240" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6252" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd97bb56-c6ba-56e1-a118-da23ec935a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328116} {:answer/id #uuid "fecad2d3-775c-5b11-a6db-515d5ea7f267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328117} {:answer/id #uuid "42c4cde9-c8de-50ef-a9f9-58f74be1be94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328118} {:answer/id #uuid "647dc9d1-24cd-5c0c-9dd5-912079a1277a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328119} {:answer/id #uuid "a50dbf58-3a9c-5229-ac9d-8301c2cb350c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fecad2d3-775c-5b11-a6db-515d5ea7f267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328117} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe12377-8471-56c4-bd37-d7487894b69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff7ae082-6b60-5021-9c27-7d6fc5b0e34f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122698" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t}+1,\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2, \\\\ y & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)),\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))}{3{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}{3{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{3{e}^{2\\cdot 0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,0)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ is the $y$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c4564a8-9902-5302-96d5-05e200d8013e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328123} {:answer/id #uuid "86f13dc2-3b60-550d-878f-52f156c64725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328124} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e64f55-7bcf-5785-8d5c-94cd8b750d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328125} {:answer/id #uuid "8cee3db1-9b31-5563-af67-589936eb35d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328126} {:answer/id #uuid "977392ff-6fb5-5a8a-9f6f-2ef1f88fb65f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f13dc2-3b60-550d-878f-52f156c64725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328124} :answer-field/id #uuid "350ef775-dd25-5e37-aaa5-22a1907149ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "430b8b34-04c8-52df-bdc6-a0f2f0e396d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122898" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3t\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-3}{\\frac{2}{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{3}-3t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{3}-3(1)}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{3}{2})\\,}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f07e773-26f2-50c1-91ae-7b5f27852d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328130} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb41d8e-f957-53e7-b18b-6ee006245371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328131} {:answer/id #uuid "42dc512a-ad60-5da4-9b5b-47c0a03ded2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328132} {:answer/id #uuid "9f33c913-b9a1-58a8-aafd-788c84cf79c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328133} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6fe34a-5405-57c7-acd4-bcd7e6f5617d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42dc512a-ad60-5da4-9b5b-47c0a03ded2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328132} :answer-field/id #uuid "93d2620f-1f6d-5040-8b0b-4b2ab060a0ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b70025b-226b-544b-a381-c6037d813ff8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122946" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c36bbe9-1359-5618-a74e-edf5d5758e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328137} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b732c1-6425-5d0f-8380-2b4345b44297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328138} {:answer/id #uuid "581c818c-c60b-5513-940f-e72fc8e87637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328139} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc7068d-f209-5dd4-9838-7a2290dccd66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328140} {:answer/id #uuid "799bf862-ae41-5d1c-8d42-b041355736d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6b732c1-6425-5d0f-8380-2b4345b44297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328138} :answer-field/id #uuid "a08bb09e-37be-5694-afb1-667e3550486f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19efb6b5-9426-572c-bf66-26e959bfbbc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122947" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi t)-2t,\\,y={e}^{2t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})=2{e}^{2\\times (1/2)}=2e\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9be2ac7-5fdd-59a2-b70c-40b4bd4cb786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{7},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328144} {:answer/id #uuid "bea35c93-97ea-51a9-8061-be15a1b089eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328145} {:answer/id #uuid "d88fd828-b456-5597-85df-f66c9c59d4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328146} {:answer/id #uuid "07ab2975-6f45-5014-a6d4-2b2d1ef8c422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328147} {:answer/id #uuid "95ada701-0cff-52d5-8c80-2c08f142ed3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{9},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea35c93-97ea-51a9-8061-be15a1b089eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328145} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b451a3c-d09b-569f-b68b-2e254be9f79a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29f052c6-c270-5aa0-8344-16e349b88fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-15564" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}=1+0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=0+1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(\\frac{4}{3},1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{4}{3},1)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ -1 & =x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)$ is the $x$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f6ae3b2-630d-5ba6-b03f-f2bd215dc2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084772} {:answer/id #uuid "e80b527e-cb3c-5a74-b5c0-0aeac4525afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084773} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7a0050-2e1f-5220-a412-a2c3dad870a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084774} {:answer/id #uuid "572b1132-3c3f-52cd-b7e2-87c093f3ba29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084775} {:answer/id #uuid "7473bed6-7d3f-5fb2-a6ae-05b81ff46510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f7a0050-2e1f-5220-a412-a2c3dad870a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084774} :answer-field/id #uuid "190540d5-34c4-5fc1-bc2e-ddb4e0d89573" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbab7bcf-6a8b-5a33-ae85-61d0acf13fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15570" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9ba7e1c-d785-511e-981c-1680eead2835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328151} {:answer/id #uuid "96f6a909-444e-509b-980d-3db4763ed0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328152} {:answer/id #uuid "106961b3-6b50-5e77-970c-ade213532dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328153} {:answer/id #uuid "96c75a6f-41fb-5252-b22e-e925b86d7ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328154} {:answer/id #uuid "465faa5f-21c0-5624-bcbc-15c6e69eb750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96f6a909-444e-509b-980d-3db4763ed0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328152} :answer-field/id #uuid "330f0c30-040a-5b5d-9aac-fb07db5dca33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83179f5c-6e11-5515-9a0e-188d40ccc82d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20853" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{′}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0fa5cb-58b2-5991-aeb3-3c72630b91f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085058} {:answer/id #uuid "d40ce140-4fa5-5e19-a6d2-c2ed4122946e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085059} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdb6ae8-5736-5ecb-a2b0-06e71866c8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{3π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085060} {:answer/id #uuid "1f118b88-363d-549d-8d78-43f7f7a3fe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085061} {:answer/id #uuid "416acf6e-914c-5f39-8409-93bf2a0d33b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d40ce140-4fa5-5e19-a6d2-c2ed4122946e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085059} :answer-field/id #uuid "b486e99f-74f6-58c2-873a-74bcf30e0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbd878d8-e318-5109-beb9-89ecefca7f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-21081" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a418921-4ddc-515d-be59-063b11f1b91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085070} {:answer/id #uuid "c626ea12-f712-5a6d-abb3-d3c9454f17e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085071} {:answer/id #uuid "dbef1b02-004e-5d07-ac10-f99bb4bdf640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085072} {:answer/id #uuid "7265d5ee-6cfb-5775-a948-08318ea9de4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085073} {:answer/id #uuid "7b461d49-e04e-5a80-b9a1-1a16086bbb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7265d5ee-6cfb-5775-a948-08318ea9de4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085073} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8768f88-2f81-52cb-99c3-baa01a649588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad8de0d6-0fb0-5332-a3cd-f5bf44efff31" :question/math-academy-id "q-21207" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cdb695-341c-5869-a750-a6eeeed896cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cdb695-341c-5869-a750-a6eeeed896cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328158} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83e753d-01f2-5c37-8053-3beebf304df1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c84a1a58-ffd9-56ee-a3b8-04aed5e73d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348109" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{3}-4(2)=8-8=0 \\\\ y & =2(2)^{2}+1=2(4)+1=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,9).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{3{t}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{4(2)}{3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3(4)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-9 & =x \\\\ y & =x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb9ffbbc-82f7-5087-a26e-121a53e06471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9ffbbc-82f7-5087-a26e-121a53e06471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328161} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d2e78c-13f9-5dcf-9b01-be7664af1dcb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43e70bd0-e92a-5815-844f-5acaf51122a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43e70bd0-e92a-5815-844f-5acaf51122a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328163} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eba9c9c-e598-56dc-9a36-2a764a59a1ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "917cc07e-7c6c-5b76-ae6f-472870182042" :question/math-academy-id "q-348115" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)=3e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3e,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,e} & =\\frac{1}{e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3e,1)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4e}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1=\\frac{1}{4e}(x-3e).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{1}{4e}(0-3e) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "616cf01b-42d0-5398-9319-1677eaae1af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "616cf01b-42d0-5398-9319-1677eaae1af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328166} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81fd662-a2da-5909-9235-44ffc4e05fc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee186c82-2942-5353-9be7-a213161b973f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee186c82-2942-5353-9be7-a213161b973f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328168} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c669d61-c30a-5583-b15c-acdfb974c3fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e73fc07-6c36-55be-b36c-099a85861c24" :question/math-academy-id "q-350359" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{2}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2eaef25-fa40-5474-aa93-94d7dc5679c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2eaef25-fa40-5474-aa93-94d7dc5679c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328171} :answer-field/id #uuid "91466fa3-7d86-54a5-b315-bd73faedc724" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "307d88f7-62e2-560a-8ec2-6717b4a2e408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "307d88f7-62e2-560a-8ec2-6717b4a2e408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328173} :answer-field/id #uuid "cca0e8a3-bd4a-513a-a193-de1493cad4eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87619e0a-4cfe-5be8-a034-9a2f3a043e1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-350368" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=-1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5635d8-8141-5a78-909f-d5cf0dfa92d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e5635d8-8141-5a78-909f-d5cf0dfa92d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328176} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d2dd08-1509-5e0c-9b20-97122d754d7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f6570b1-9b17-57ae-9d89-8d0a757a3443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6570b1-9b17-57ae-9d89-8d0a757a3443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328178} :answer-field/id #uuid "77d53214-4ef4-5804-af58-b61ec8fd788c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69d258b7-b3e5-504c-8494-6e597182b1b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-350386" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,-2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =-4 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(-3,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "908fb5cc-8476-5501-9840-cd1336cca7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328181} {:answer/id #uuid "06f68193-9b19-5676-a623-e31e132823d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "908fb5cc-8476-5501-9840-cd1336cca7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328181} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dfce342-0693-5a98-b511-10ab7720408d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e47c44e8-19b1-5575-9ec4-aba3b22f4668" :question/math-academy-id "q-350403" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =8 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(4) & =6{(4)}^{2}=96\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2ec566e-7b0e-5b64-9f02-1f11c2dfe259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2ec566e-7b0e-5b64-9f02-1f11c2dfe259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328185} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d882c6-a6af-5962-8f97-eb32740b22f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328184} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61dafa77-da17-5b62-bd84-847d9b6b5b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61dafa77-da17-5b62-bd84-847d9b6b5b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328187} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a5d72c-32bc-57c6-8bb3-e1ef37308249" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30d2aed7-7b3f-5c5c-aec5-e9ecfd820e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-350414" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "326352f5-3012-5dc9-a3d1-131cdf74ec4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328190} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c573bc-d647-5a9b-bd72-49595c7f390d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "326352f5-3012-5dc9-a3d1-131cdf74ec4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328190} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d85e419-b9be-540e-bf61-c5220a7f6ec3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8af08ec-710f-5106-800d-33b2a65b25b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8af08ec-710f-5106-800d-33b2a65b25b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328193} :answer-field/id #uuid "536444a5-22cf-575c-b95c-99eb96d3bebe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31b3faa4-b461-5004-916d-d8b763ad8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-357255" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc91621-aeb5-5b9b-8967-1863095c9fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = (x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086216} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f0b63-39aa-55ed-bf75-6b620acc0edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = 2(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086217} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e80624-b2f0-5ef1-92d0-df5481905b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{24}{11}x + \\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086218} {:answer/id #uuid "0613faa1-bfa6-50e0-8304-289efb1c481e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = 2(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086219} {:answer/id #uuid "31b8a3af-c940-5339-b1e9-dd6b2e0c4f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = (x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0613faa1-bfa6-50e0-8304-289efb1c481e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = 2(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086219} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f78337f-4f92-5159-96b2-5bbb8f36eda9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b94e72be-f23a-535e-b719-cf664553611b" :question/math-academy-id "q-6857" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}-5,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=2-3=-1, \\\\ y & =3(1)^{2}-5=3-5=-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{6{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot (1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6-3} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =2(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3b15a64-f05a-5f1f-87c8-cbcbdc4dc9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{12}{5}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328196} {:answer/id #uuid "7f970631-df88-573f-9cac-544fd888abb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328197} {:answer/id #uuid "1d103378-e181-5a90-9206-e3134a0e8e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328198} {:answer/id #uuid "98f0488f-760b-5f04-b18d-7e054e2fd002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328199} {:answer/id #uuid "1ddbc08a-8d73-57f2-b1d2-8cd3d416345a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d103378-e181-5a90-9206-e3134a0e8e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328198} :answer-field/id #uuid "84a9bb93-6316-547f-a2a0-5b05303dbe54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f364007-9bf0-5b90-a6c7-48aa10ff5533" :question/math-academy-id "q-6858" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=2-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={0}^{3}-3\\cdot 0=0-0=0, \\\\ y & =2-0=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{1}{3})\\,}=-3\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-3(x-0) \\\\ y & =-3x+2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "465fc6ae-5f70-5113-8756-3dd823b5db45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328203} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ac7c90-4ceb-5022-a4e2-d650909dad8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328204} {:answer/id #uuid "7905fa6a-a016-5778-a69c-417067276cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328205} {:answer/id #uuid "805b9085-3647-58bc-8845-4bb350242f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x-2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328206} {:answer/id #uuid "34619916-ae96-5930-8c4c-86d577bb5586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7905fa6a-a016-5778-a69c-417067276cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328205} :answer-field/id #uuid "31a5b764-1919-5096-879d-6f83334acb59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f065fb78-5c08-5a67-9bfa-56a34450ed2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75288" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
